WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.217
NARRATOR: When two measured variables change
together, their data can show a correlation.

2
00:00:05.217 --> 00:00:10.331
NARRATOR: A positive relationship means they
tend to rise together; a negative relationship

3
00:00:10.331 --> 00:00:13.872
means one tends to fall as the other rises.

4
00:00:13.872 --> 00:00:16.182
NARRATOR: Correlation describes a pattern.

5
00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.415
NARRATOR: It does not yet explain why the
pattern exists.

6
00:00:19.538 --> 00:00:24.080
NARRATOR: Ice-cream sales and reported crime
can both rise during hot weather.

7
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.436
NARRATOR: That does not mean buying ice cream
causes crime.

8
00:00:27.436 --> 00:00:32.218
NARRATOR: Temperature changes behavior in ways
that can affect both measurements.

9
00:00:32.218 --> 00:00:36.727
NARRATOR: This third-variable problem is one
reason an observed relationship cannot

10
00:00:36.727 --> 00:00:39.596
establish a causal direction on its own.

11
00:00:39.722 --> 00:00:45.716
NARRATOR: To support a causal claim,
researchers need a design that manipulates an

12
00:00:45.716 --> 00:00:50.788
independent variable and controls competing
explanations, often with comparison groups and

13
00:00:50.788 --> 00:00:51.710
random assignment.

14
00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:56.886
NARRATOR: Observational correlations remain
valuable for prediction and discovery.

15
00:00:56.886 --> 00:01:01.815
NARRATOR: The professional habit is to make a
claim no stronger than the study design

16
00:01:01.815 --> 00:01:02.144
allows.
